;; Incompleteness of congruence closure modulo associativity alone.
;;
;; Cat is associative, not commutative. Two asserted equalities:
;;   Cat[a, b] = x      and      Cat[b, c] = y
;; Completion orients both toward the shorter side (shortlex):
;;   Cat[a, b] -> x,  Cat[b, c] -> y
;; The word Cat[a, b, c] rewrites two ways, overlapping on b:
;;   Cat[a, b, c] -> Cat[x, c]   and   Cat[a, b, c] -> Cat[a, y]
;; so Cat[x, c] = Cat[a, y] is a consequence. Both sides are normal forms
;; of the two rules, and the only derivation between them goes through
;; the longer word Cat[a, b, c]. The engine's A-only completion reduces
;; contiguous factors and never superposes (a_round, egraph.rs ~2420), so
;; it does not add the critical-pair rule and the check fails.
;;
;; Adding superposition would close this instance, but not the problem:
;; ground congruence closure modulo associativity is the word problem for
;; finitely presented semigroups, undecidable (Markov 1947, Post 1947),
;; so no terminating completion is complete for every program.
(sort E)
(function a () E) (function b () E) (function c () E)
(function x () E) (function y () E)
(function Cat (E) E :assoc)
(set-option :completion eager)
(union (Cat a b) (x))
(union (Cat b c) (y))
;; Variant: the longer word Cat[a, b, c] is materialized as a node. The
;; check still fails: reduction rewrites the node to one normal form and
;; merges it there; the second reduct is never produced because the rules
;; are not confluent and no critical pair is computed.
(let w (Cat a b c))
(let lhs (Cat x c))
(let rhs (Cat a y))
(check-eq lhs rhs)
